# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: comparison-operators-vendor-semantics — probe results for = /= < <= > >= versus the spec
#+STATUS: DONE (clautolisp 2.2.90 / alfe 2.2.85 / spec 0.14.0, 2026-09-25)
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: claude/dreamy-ptolemy-uj8mzd

* Context

pjb (2026-09-25): the spec is the golden rule, but where implementations
differ each dialect reproduces its vendor, with a dialect warning on any
divergence (between vendors, or between the spec and a vendor);
clautolisp itself implements the most useful behaviour. So: probe first,
compare, then decide.

Probe: =autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/language/comparison-operators-probe.lsp=
(clautolisp 2.2.89) — 40 argument cases × 6 operators = 240 observations,
=OBSERVE <op>.<case> T|nil|error=, op ∈ eq(=) ne(/=) lt le gt ge.

* Runs received (2026-09-25)

| target                      | result                                          |
|-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------|
| BricsCAD, macOS (V26)       | 240 observations                                |
| BricsCAD V25 fr_FR, Windows | 240 observations, IDENTICAL to macOS            |
| AutoCAD 2022, Windows       | first run: NO output (only the alfe COM-ProgID warning); re-run: 240 observations |
| clautolisp macOS / Windows  | an old build (before 2.2.87: no string ordering) — superseded by the Linux 2.2.89 run |

* Findings (BricsCAD, first pass)

** = and /=  — no type error at all

BricsCAD agrees with clautolisp on every === case and every =/== case but
one:
- =(= 1 "1")= → nil, =(= 1 "a")= → nil — NOT a type error (spec says error).
- =(= 'a 'a)= → T, =(= 'a 'b)= → nil, =(= T T)= → T; same list object → T,
  =equal= lists → nil: identity for non-numbers/non-strings (spec says
  symbols are out of domain → error). alfe's bootstrap relies on this.
- =(= nil nil)= → T (spec says nil).
- =(= "a" "A")= → nil (case-sensitive).
- =(/= 1 2 1)= → *T* in BricsCAD, nil in clautolisp: BricsCAD's =/= tests
  ADJACENT pairs (like =<=), clautolisp tests ALL pairs.
- =(=)=, =(/=)= → error "too few / too many arguments" (clautolisp: error too).
- one argument: =(= x)=, =(/= x)= → T for any x, nil included.

** < <= > >= — nil is the smallest value, not bottom

The spec's "any nil argument → nil (⊥ propagation)" is WRONG for
BricsCAD. BricsCAD orders nil BELOW numbers and strings:

| case           | <     | <=    | >     | >=    |
|----------------+-------+-------+-------+-------|
| (op nil nil)   | nil   | T     | nil   | T     |
| (op 1 nil)     | nil   | nil   | T     | T     |
| (op nil 1)     | T     | T     | nil   | nil   |
| (op "a" nil)   | nil   | nil   | T     | T     |
| (op 'a nil)    | nil   | nil   | T     | error |
| (op 1 "a" nil) | error | error | error | error |
| (op nil 1 "a") | error | error | error | error |

(clautolisp 2.2.88: nil in every one of these, no error.) The spec's own
evidence rows — =(< 1 nil) = nil=, =(< "abc" nil) = nil= — are consistent
with nil-is-smallest, so the ⊥ reading was an over-generalisation. The
SCHMS loop guard =(<= 48 nil 57)= still gives nil (48 <= nil is false).
=(> 'a nil)= → T but =(>= 'a nil)= → error looks like an implementation
accident (>= checked differently from >).

A chain is evaluated left to right with no nil short-circuit: an
incompatible pair anywhere signals, even with a nil elsewhere.

Other relational rows:
- number vs string → error: "bad argument type <"a"> ; expected <NUMBER>"
  (or "<1> ; expected <STRING>" when the string comes first) — agrees with
  the spec and clautolisp 2.2.88.
- symbols, T, lists (2+ arguments) → error "expected <NUMBER>" — agrees.
- strings order by code point, case-sensitive, prefix first — agrees.
- one argument: T for ANY type (=(< 'a)= → T, =(< nil)= → T): no type
  check; clautolisp 2.2.88 signals on =(< 'a)= and returns nil on =(< nil)=.
- no argument: =(<)= → error "too few / too many arguments"; clautolisp
  returns T.

* AutoCAD 2022 (French, Windows) — 237/240 identical to BricsCAD

AutoCAD agrees with BricsCAD everywhere except three rows:

| case             | AutoCAD 2022 | BricsCAD V25/V26 |
|------------------+--------------+------------------|
| (>= 'a nil)      | T            | error            |
| (> nil 1 "a")    | nil          | error            |
| (>= nil 1 "a")   | nil          | error            |

AutoCAD compares the chain pair by pair, left to right, and STOPS at the
first false pair: (> nil 1 "a") never reaches 1 vs "a". BricsCAD raises
the error anyway, and treats (>= 'a nil) differently from (> 'a nil).
(< nil 1 "a") errors on both: nil < 1 is true, so 1 vs "a" is reached.

AutoCAD error texts (French build):
- "type d'argument de comparaison incorrect: 1 \"a\"" — names the pair.
- "nombre d'arguments insuffisants" — for (=), (<), ...

* The vendor model (both vendors, AutoCAD where they differ)

- === / =/==: any types, never an error. Numbers by value across
  int/real, strings by content (case-sensitive), anything else by
  identity (eq). =(= nil nil)= → T. =/== tests ADJACENT pairs:
  =(/= 1 2 1)= → T.
- =<= =<== =>= =>==: nil is the SMALLEST value — below every number,
  string and symbol; nil equals nil. Two non-nil arguments must both be
  numbers or both strings, else a type error naming the pair.
- All six: pairwise left to right, stopping at the first false pair;
  no argument → error "too few arguments"; one argument → T, whatever its
  type (no check).

* To decide

For each row: spec vs AutoCAD vs BricsCAD vs clautolisp → divergence case
(AGENTS.md § Dialect Divergence), per-dialect behaviour + warning, and
what clautolisp does. Then correct the spec (= accepts any type by
identity; nil ordering; arity; /= pairing), wire the probe as conformance
scenarios with per-target expectations, and change the code.

* Decision (pjb, 2026-09-25)

Agreed as proposed: the spec follows what both vendors do (case 0, no
warning); AutoCAD is adopted for the three rows where they differ (case
2); clautolisp's own dialect gives AutoCAD's answers.

* Resolution (clautolisp 2.2.90 / alfe 2.2.85 / autolisp-spec 0.14.0)

- Code (=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=):
  =relational-order-p= replaces =numeric-order-p= — nil is the smallest
  value, adjacent pairs left to right stopping at the first false one,
  no argument an error, one argument T, other non-nil mixtures a type
  error (=:invalid-comparison-argument=). =/== compares adjacent pairs.
  The three divergent rows go through =%resolved-comparison-policy=:
  bricscad → BricsCAD's error + =[comparison-divergence]= warning;
  strict → AutoCAD's answer + warning; autocad / clautolisp / lax →
  AutoCAD's answer, silent. A 2-argument comparison never consults the
  dialect except =(>= x nil)= with x neither a number nor a string.
- Verified with the built binary: under =--dialect bricscad= the probe
  reproduces BricsCAD on all 240 observations; under strict / autocad /
  clautolisp / lax it reproduces AutoCAD on all 240.
- Spec 0.14.0: "The Comparison Operators" section, the six Function
  Entries, the Equality Predicates page (ch.3) and the NIL entry (ch.4)
  rewritten from the probe; =*** clautolisp= notes under =>= and =>==.
- Tests: =comparison-operators-match-autocad-probe= (all 240 AutoCAD
  observations), =comparison-operators-bricscad-divergences= (the three
  rows × five dialects, with warnings), =comparison-operators-type-error-code=,
  =numeric-order-nil-is-the-smallest-value=; the older =/== and nil-fold
  assertions updated; the compiler's open-coded corpus extended.
- Conformance scenarios: =autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/language/=
  =comparison-operators{,-bricscad,-autocad}.sexp=, generated by
  =generate-comparison-scenarios.lisp=. The clautolisp one passes (240
  conform); the received vendor outputs classify as BricsCAD 237 conform
  + 3 known-divergence, AutoCAD 240 conform.
- Suites: clautolisp (34 suites) and alfe (1082 checks) green under SBCL;
  the autolisp-test corpus gives identical per-test results before and
  after.
- Open follow-up: whether BricsCAD also rejects =(>= '(1) nil)=,
  =(>= T nil)= — SPEC-UNCERTAIN marker + deferred-spec-research entry.
